      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(3)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=4.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
