      ":question/id": "2d2e6e66-d32a-5c8a-b80d-918fcb1ac806",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=5$\n- **III.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
