    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7c1419bc-9bdb-59ea-a7d6-3219cfb4e8ff",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=0,\\,{y}^{″}(4)=1$\n- **II.** $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(3)=3$\n- **III.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(4)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=0,\\,{y}^{″}(4)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(3)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(3)=3.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "71a7d1fc-49a0-5442-b120-8af6ca4ffe41",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
